		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: Collision on a Client/Server system - practical?]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=11894#p11894"><![CDATA[
<a href="http://gafferongames.wordpress.com/2008/02/27/gdc-slides-released/#comment-10129" class="postlink">http://gafferongames.wordpress.com/2008 ... ment-10129</a> is interesting (scroll up to top of page for blog post, the comment linked has a working link to the slides).<br><br>Of course the big issue for a multiplayer game is cheating. If you don't have to worry about that, then you can be more flexible. It's unclear to me that this talk deals with cheating (since he mentions it's for Co-op stuff). This is fine if your situation can deal with it. (And remember - if anything is indeterminant, being cheat-resistant will catch this as well, since "client does wrong math" is indistinguishable from "client is cheating" on the server's end.)<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=3004">bengarney</a> — Mon Dec 22, 2008 4:51 am</p><hr />
]]></content>
